      "text": " To solve the sum, we can use the fact that the sum converges. Since the denominator is monotonically increasing and the numerator is monotonically decreasing, we can write\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= \\frac{1}{x^x} + \\frac{1}{x^{x/(x - 1)}} + \\frac{1}{x^{x/(x^2 - 1)}} + \\dotsb \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^x} + \\frac{x^x}{x^x - x^{1/(x - 1)}} + \\frac{x^x}{x^x - x^{1/(x^2 - 1)}} + \\dotsb.\n\\end{align*}If we subtract $\\frac{1}{x^x}$ from each side, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= \\frac{1}{x^x - x^{1/x}} + \\frac{x^x - x^{1/(x - 1)}}{x^x - x^{1/x}} + \\frac{x^x - x^{1/(x^2 - 1)}}{x^x - x^{1/x}} + \\dotsb\\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{1/x}} + \\frac{1}{x^{1/1 - 1}} + \\frac{1}{x^{1/(1^2 - 1)}} + \\dotsb\\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{1/x}} + 1 + 1 + \\dotsb.\n\\end{align*}The last sum diverges to $\\infty,$ so we should expect the original sum to diverge as well. The solution diverges.",
